import json,os
R=json.load(open('refit_results.json'))
labels=['TwoScaleStick','ProjectionStick','PeriodicContact']
ref='Related friction-model background: B. Armstrong-Helouvry, P. Dupont and C. Canudas de Wit, A survey of models, analysis tools and compensation methods for the control of machines with friction, Automatica 30 (1994), 1083-1138; bibliographic reference recalled from memory, not source-checked. These expressions are phenomenological reductions, not the LuGre model.'
D0='a*u-g*sin(theta)'
Dh='a*u-g*sin(theta)*(1+h*cos(theta))-b*omega'
expr={
'TwoScaleStick':D0+'-b*omega-c*tanh(omega/v)-exp(-(omega/vs)**2)*Min(Max('+D0+',-c),c)',
'ProjectionStick':Dh+'-Min(Max('+Dh+'+omega/tau,-c-d),c-d)',
'PeriodicContact':D0+'-b*omega-c*(1+h*sin(theta)+j*cos(theta))*tanh(omega/v)-exp(-(omega/vs)**2)*Min(Max('+D0+',-(c+delta)),c+delta)'}
keys={'TwoScaleStick':['a','g','b','c','v','vs'],'ProjectionStick':['a','g','b','c','tau','d','h'],'PeriodicContact':['a','g','b','c','v','delta','vs','h','j']}
units={'a':'rad/(s^2 V)','g':'rad/s^2','b':'1/s','c':'rad/s^2','v':'rad/s','vs':'rad/s','tau':'s','d':'rad/s^2','delta':'rad/s^2','h':'-','j':'-'}
desc={'a':'Motor-voltage to acceleration gain `a`; only gain divided by inertia is identified.','g':'Positive restoring-acceleration scale `g`.','b':'Positive viscous acceleration coefficient `b`; includes motor back-emf damping and bearing friction without separating them.','c':'Dry-friction acceleration scale `c`.','v':'Sliding-friction regularization speed `v` in `tanh(omega/v)`.','vs':'Independent static-friction localization speed `vs` in `exp(-(omega/vs)**2)`.','tau':'Torque-projection relaxation time `tau`; this is an effective low-speed regularization time, not an identified motor electrical time constant.','d':'Directional-friction half-difference `d`; the projection interval is `[-c-d,c-d]` in acceleration units.','delta':'Nonnegative excess static threshold `delta`; the independent static bound is `c+delta`.','h':'Fitted angular-modulation coefficient `h`; its precise use is stated in the interpretation.','j':'Fitted cosine modulation coefficient `j` of sliding-friction magnitude.'}
family={
'TwoScaleStick':'Regularized Coulomb-viscous friction with a static dead zone and independent sliding/static speed scales. '+ref,
'ProjectionStick':'Coulomb-viscous differential-inclusion approximation by interval projection and a low-speed relaxation. Related, but not identical, to D. Karnopp, Computer Simulation of Stick-Slip Friction in Mechanical Dynamic Systems, Journal of Dynamic Systems, Measurement, and Control 107 (1985), 100-103; reference recalled from memory, not source-checked. This submitted acceleration-only projection law is our explicit regularization, not an assertion that Karnopp published this exact expression.',
'PeriodicContact':'Nonlinear pendulum state-space model with angle-modulated regularized Coulomb-viscous friction and a static dead zone. '+ref}
interpret={
'TwoScaleStick':'The mandatory kinematics are `dtheta = omega`. Motor torque per inertia is `a*u`; hanging-equilibrium restoring torque per inertia is `-g*sin(theta)`; `-b*omega` is viscous and back-emf damping. The sliding term is `-c*tanh(omega/v)`. Define the available acceleration `D = a*u-g*sin(theta)` and the static weight `H = exp(-(omega/vs)**2)`. The countertorque term is `-H*Min(Max(D,-c),c)`: at rest it cancels any available acceleration inside `[-c,c]`, whereas away from rest it vanishes. The two speed scales are separate because sliding-force smoothing and localization of static cancellation serve different purposes. Both friction and gravity coefficients are normalized by an unknown inertia. At `omega = 0` and `u = 0`, `D-Min(Max(D,-c),c)` has the same sign as `D`, or is zero; therefore R2 holds. Since `0 <= H <= 1`, the acceleration is nondecreasing in `u` with slope between `a*(1-H)` and `a`, including plateau regions; this establishes R3 in the nondecreasing sense required for sticking.',
'ProjectionStick':'The kinematics are `dtheta = omega`. Define available acceleration `D = a*u-g*sin(theta)*(1+h*cos(theta))-b*omega`. The motor term is `a*u`, damping is `-b*omega`, and the restoring term includes a second angular harmonic through `sin(theta)*cos(theta)`. Define `Z = D+omega/tau`. The opposing acceleration is the projection `Min(Max(Z,-c-d),c-d)`, allowing different positive and negative sliding magnitudes. Inside the projection interval, `domega = -omega/tau`, so velocity decays without requiring a discontinuous velocity sign and every zero-velocity state inside the static interval is an exact equilibrium. Outside it, the limiting Coulomb countertorque applies. This is a mathematical low-speed relaxation approximation, not a measured internal friction state. Positive `g` and `Abs(h)<1` ensure that restoring torque opposes `theta` on the required domain. The interval contains zero because `c>Abs(d)`, so projection cannot reverse the sign of the residual available acceleration at rest: R2 holds. As `u` increases, projection slope is zero or one; consequently acceleration slope is zero or `a`, establishing R3 in the nondecreasing sense.',
'PeriodicContact':'The kinematics are `dtheta = omega`. The terms `a*u`, `-g*sin(theta)` and `-b*omega` are respectively motor gain, restoring acceleration and viscous/back-emf damping. The sliding-friction term is `-c*(1+h*sin(theta)+j*cos(theta))*tanh(omega/v)`. Its angular factors change the magnitude, not the direction, of the resisting force; no experimentally established cause is assigned to this modulation. Its magnitude stays positive because `Abs(h)+Abs(j)<1`. Define `D = a*u-g*sin(theta)` and `H = exp(-(omega/vs)**2)`. Static cancellation is `-H*Min(Max(D,-(c+delta)),c+delta)`; the static threshold is independent of the angular sliding modulation. At rest the cancellation leaves the sign of `D` unchanged or zero, ensuring R2. The acceleration slope with respect to `u` is between `a*(1-H)` and `a`, ensuring R3 in the nondecreasing sense. The static and sliding speed scales have independent roles.'}
origin={
'TwoScaleStick':'The motor gain, sinusoidal restoring term, viscous damping and Coulomb friction came from physical reasoning. Moving-sample ordinary least squares in explore_data.py gave initial physical scales; subsequent fits minimized uncorrected angle simulations rather than noisy acceleration. A single-scale Gaussian static cancellation was tried in fit_models.py; it preferred a static threshold smaller than the observed stationary torque band and drifted in extended rest tests. Independent static/sliding scales and a nonnegative static excess were then tried in fit_stiction.py. After pooled refinement the Stribeck excess was nearly zero, so it was removed, both the speed-localized sliding excess and static excess were set to zero, and the remaining six parameters were refitted in fit_twoscale.py. Thus the final equality of sliding and static thresholds is an explicit simplifying assumption, not a separately measured fact. The tanh and Gaussian operators were chosen regularizers, not symbolic-regression discoveries. No genetic-programming or PySINDy search was run.',
'ProjectionStick':'The motor, viscous, pendulum and Coulomb terms came from physical reasoning. Acceleration residuals and free-response errors motivated a modest second harmonic, first tested in fit_models.py. Direction-dependent residual means motivated asymmetric Coulomb limits. Smooth single-scale static models showed unwanted long-rest drift. The projection operator was designed as a distinct force-limited low-speed family: it cancels the available torque inside the static interval while relaxing velocity. The interval operators and `omega/tau` regularizer are an explicit phenomenological construction, not a symbolic-search result. Trial projection structures with and without an angular harmonic or motor saturation are recorded in fit_projection.py; the harmonic variant was retained for its better training angle simulation and correct persistence at rest.',
'PeriodicContact':'Linear motor gain, sine restoring torque, viscous damping and dry friction were physically motivated. Training moving-state residuals were largest at negative angles beyond approximately `theta = -2` rad; this motivated both altered restoring torque and angle-dependent sliding friction. Low-order `sin(theta)` and `cos(theta)` modulation was a physically constrained hypothesis for nonuniform contact, not evidence that disc runout or pad geometry was measured. It reduced these residuals and training/free-validation simulation errors without changing the direction of sliding friction. Independent Gaussian static localization and tanh sliding smoothing were retained to sustain the observed rest band. Higher-order polynomial input, quadratic drag, Stribeck weakening, and single-scale directional models were also tried; their fits did not justify retaining them in this pool. No symbolic-regression search was used.'}
limitations={
'TwoScaleStick':'This smooth approximation is not an exact set-valued friction law away from zero velocity. Independent low-speed widths may admit extra low-speed force-balance branches; they are effective regularizers. Motor inductance, friction memory and temperature are omitted because the required model has only the two recorded states. The fitted gravity scale, motor gain and friction are normalized by inertia, which these data cannot separately identify. Input excitation occupies a narrower range than the formal voltage domain, so extrapolation to large voltage is uncertain. Large negative-angle free-response phase error remains. Cross-validation was used in model selection and is not an independent final test.',
'ProjectionStick':'The fitted `tau` is near its imposed lower bound, so a physical relaxation time is not established. The bounds were chosen to avoid resolving a much faster low-speed decay than the specified integration step. The model is piecewise smooth and changing the simulation method or sample period may change the best effective parameters. The second harmonic is an empirical torque-shape correction; the data do not distinguish added spring torque from other errors. Hysteresis, true motor electrical dynamics and separate static-versus-kinetic friction magnitudes are omitted. Motor gain, damping and friction are divided by unidentified inertia. Large-angle and voltage extrapolation beyond the observed recordings is uncertain; cross-validation informed selection rather than furnishing an untouched test.',
'PeriodicContact':'Angular modulation is phenomenological and may absorb trajectory-dependent errors rather than fixed contact geometry. Static threshold is constant although sliding magnitude varies with angle; it is not constrained to exceed every local sliding magnitude. The fitted static excess is near zero and not separately established. This relatively flexible structure had substantially different errors among driven trajectories, indicating possible overfitting. Low-speed relaxation, encoder quantization and any hysteresis are only approximated. Motor and friction coefficients are normalized by unknown inertia; electrical memory and temperature are absent. Do not extrapolate the angular modulation as verified torque measurements or treat cross-validation used for selection as an independent test.'}
def numbertext(names,values):
 return ', '.join('`'+k+' = '+format(float(v),'.12g')+'`'+(' '+units[k] if units[k]!='-' else '') for k,v in zip(names,values))
os.makedirs('models',exist_ok=True)
for label in labels:
 r=R[label];names=keys[label];rows=[{'name':k,'value':v,'unit':units[k],'type':'fitted','description':desc[k]} for k,v in zip(names,r['p'])]
 starts='; '.join(numbertext(names,start) for start in r['starts'])
 boundtext='; '.join('`'+format(float(lo),'.12g')+' <= '+k+' <= '+format(float(hi),'.12g')+'`'+(' '+units[k] if units[k]!='-' else '') for k,lo,hi in zip(names,*r['bounds']))
 script='fit_twoscale.py' if label=='TwoScaleStick' else 'refit_pool.py'
 diag=' Pooled three-fold trajectory cross-validation angle RMSE was '+format(r['cv_rmse'],'.6f')+' rad. After pooled refitting, angle RMSE was '+format(r['pooled'][0],'.6f')+' rad across both supplied files; this is in-sample, not held-out performance.'
 method='All fitted parameters listed here were estimated jointly by bounded nonlinear least squares, scipy.optimize.least_squares with its trust-region reflective algorithm, minimizing the stated full-trajectory criterion. This accommodates the nonlinear simulation objective; direct derivative regression was not used for the final values because central-difference acceleration is noisy. Three deterministic starts were tried and the lowest angle sum of squares was retained. Starting values, in parameter units: '+starts+'. Bounds: '+boundtext+'. These broad physical/regularization bounds enforce positive dissipation, admissible restoring shape and manageable low-speed stiffness; they are assumptions, not inferred confidence intervals. Finite-difference relative step was `diff_step = 0.0001`; tolerances were `ftol = 1e-9`, `xtol = 1e-9`, `gtol = 1e-9`; limit was 300 function evaluations per start. No stochastic optimization was used, so no random seed applies. Classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta used one `0.01` s step per sample and the logged `u` was held constant between samples, exactly as specified. '+('These starts were chosen around the moving-state physical scales, with three sliding/static width choices to expose local minima.' if label=='TwoScaleStick' else 'The first start was the training-only fit of this structure stored in '+('projection_results.json, family 31' if label=='ProjectionStick' else 'stiction_results.json, family 23')+'; the other starts changed regularization values as explicitly listed above to probe local minima. The earlier fits are only starts; every delivered coefficient was re-estimated together on pooled data.')+' Deterministic trajectory cross-validation used three folds with concatenated trajectory indices `[0,3,5,8,11]`, `[1,4,7,10,13]` and `[2,6,9,12,14,15]` (zero-based indices, training trajectories first then validation trajectories). Each fold restarted from the physical initialization recorded by the script, used 180 function evaluations and `1e-8` stopping tolerances; fold data were never used in that fold objective.'+diag
 data='All 3522 rows of data/train.csv (trajectories 1 through 13) and all 1192 rows of data/val.csv (trajectories 1 through 3), treated as 16 separate trajectories, never concatenated into one experiment. Final fitting uses all supplied data to improve parameter precision across both driven and free-response regimes. Initial samples initialize states and have identically zero angle residual; the remaining 4698 samples contribute to the criterion. Validation was first used to assess training-only models, then intentionally included in final fitting and trajectory cross-validation; reported post-refit validation error is therefore not independent validation.'
 lim=limitations[label]+' An additional 20 s unforced simulation initialized at each of the nine logged stationary free-response endpoints in the supplied files left every angle unchanged, supporting persistence at rest; this tests those equilibria only, not every possible low-speed state.'
 obj={'expressions':{'dtheta':'omega','domega':expr[label]},'parameters':rows,'estimation':{'criterion':'Sum of squared differences between simulated and logged `theta` across all trajectories after initialization, with equal per-sample weights. This targets the required uncorrected long-horizon angle simulation rather than a derivative proxy; no state correction is applied after the first sample.','data':data,'method':method,'script':script},'discussion':{'family':family[label],'interpretation':interpret[label],'origin':origin[label],'limitations':lim}}
 json.dump(obj,open('models/'+label+'.json','w'),indent=2)
json.dump({'order':labels,'recommended':'TwoScaleStick','reason':'`TwoScaleStick` is the six-parameter symmetric Coulomb-viscous static model with independent regularization speeds. It had the lowest three-fold trajectory cross-validation angle RMSE among the retained structures: 0.076358 rad across the supplied training and validation files, compared with 0.080005 rad for `ProjectionStick` and 0.080707 rad for `PeriodicContact`. All three preserve the nine observed stationary free-response endpoints in 20 s unforced persistence tests and analytically satisfy the restoring-sign and nondecreasing input constraints. The simpler symmetric model is recommended rather than the most flexible angular-friction structure, whose lower pooled fitting error is not independently predictive. The projection model is a distinct physically defensible sticking alternative. Scores used for selection are not untouched test scores.'},open('models/pool.json','w'),indent=2)
